Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?v0.3.2=20=E2=80=94=20onedoor=20did=20not=20work?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20on=20Windows?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two defects, both found by running the suite on a Windows machine for the
first time. Neither is reachable on Linux, which is why 111 green tests
here said nothing about them.

1. `zoneinfo` reads the operating system's IANA database. Windows does
   not ship one, so `ZoneInfo("Europe/Amsterdam")` -- and even
   `ZoneInfo("UTC")` -- raises `ZoneInfoNotFoundError`. Since cap
   windows, the engine config, the HTTP service and the MCP proxy all
   construct a `ZoneInfo`, the package could not evaluate a single
   decision on Windows. `tzdata` is now a dependency there.

2. The MCP proxy split its downstream command with `shlex.split`, which
   is POSIX by default and treats a backslash as an escape character. A
   Windows interpreter path was silently mangled --
   `C:\Users\a\.venv\Scripts\python.exe` became
   `C:Usersa.venvScriptspython.exe` -- and the spawn failed with
   WinError 2. On Windows the command string is now handed to Popen,
   which parses it with the rules Windows actually defines.

Four tests pin the splitting on both platforms, including the mangling
itself, so the POSIX branch cannot quietly become the Windows branch
again. 115 pass. mypy reports the same 7 pre-existing errors as before
this change, none in the new code.

`.gitignore` gains `dist/` and `build/`, which it should always have had.

Worth recording why this took until now: the 0.3.1 release was verified
by installing the wheel and running migrations, which passes on Windows
because it never touches a timezone. The check was narrower than the
claim it was used to support.
